  "summary_en": "Philosophical article arguing robot ethics is not relational enough. It critiques the properties approach and modest relational (as-if) approaches, and proposes an eco-relational, phenomenological approach in which properties are contextual, embodied and enacted. Human-robot co-action and non-human agency disrupt traditional ethical concepts such as autonomy and responsibility.",
  "key_provisions_or_claims": [
   "Robot ethics relies on human-exclusive properties like consciousness and intelligence as bases for ethical concepts.",
   "Properties are contextual, embodied, enacted and relational rather than possessed.",
   "Human-robot interaction yields hybrid actions (co-actions) and a form of non-human agency.",
   "Concepts like autonomy and responsibility are transformed when robots are entangled in action.",
   "Draws on African and East Asian relational traditions, animal studies and New Materialism."
  ],
